ndamentals": {"margin": 14.41, "lean": 6.0, "environment": 4.3, "incumbency": 4.0, "money": 0.11}, "rating_margin": 14.25, "sigma_race": 4.8, "sigma_total": 5.21, "baseline_margin": 13.92, "analyst_adjustment": -0.42, "key_factors": ["Consistent late-October polls show Hochul ahead by 11-14 points", "Strong Democratic fundamentals in New York plus incumbency", "Prediction markets price Hochul at 96-98%", "Blakeman's fundraising trails Hochul's with little outside support"], "rationale": "Every independent poll in the final two weeks shows Hochul leading by double digits, and fundamentals, incumbency, and a wide money edge all point the same direction. Markets and expert raters agree the race is effectively decided.", "watch": "A late surge consolidating anti-incumbent sentiment around Blakeman, or unusually low Democratic turnout in New York City, could narrow the margin more than expected.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kathy Hochul (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Kathy Hochul seeks a full term as New York's governor against Nassau County executive Bruce Blakeman. Late-October polling from Marist, Siena, and Quinnipiac consistently shows Hochul ahead by 11 to 14 points, with even Republican-sponsored surveys putting her up four to seven. The state's Democratic lean, Hochul's incumbency, and her fundraising advantage all favor her, and prediction markets price her chances at 96 percent or higher.\n\nBlakeman has campaigned on tax cuts and has drawn national attention, including surrogates like Nicki Minaj, but there is little evidence of movement in the polls. Expert raters unanimously place the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column. The main uncertainty is turnout dynamics in the final days, not the direction of the race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9859, "margin": 12.77}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9584, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9776, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9779, "